  "body": "The one product/policy pin in the #2065 design (`doc/2026-10-29T12-10-00Z_design_refundable_grants_pending_transfers/` §3, PR #2293). Filed so it is decided deliberately rather than defaulted in code.\n\n## Decision 1 — window length\n\nHow long a Steam-key grant stays pending (unspendable) before it vests.\n\n| Option | Refund exposure closed | User waits | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. 14 days (Steam's refund window)** — design recommendation | essentially all Steam refunds | up to 14 days | the rule the user experiences is the rule that protects the ledger |\n| B. 48 hours | refunds inside 48h only | 2 days | leaves a 12-day hole that looks safe and is not |\n| C. 14 days with early vest on evidence | most, sooner | hours–14 days | needs a trustworthy 'refund window closed' signal; Steam does not reliably expose one |\n| D. none (post immediately) | none | 0 | the status quo #2065 exists to replace |\n\n## Decision 2 — spendability of pending tokens\n\nRecommendation: **not spendable, at all.** Allowing purchases against pending balance reintroduces the clawback problem through the front door.\n\n## What I reviewed\n\nThe intake review on #2065 asked for this to be surfaced rather than buried; the design doc does that and carries a recommendation for each. Nothing here blocks the design doc landing — the value is a config, and the build issues read it from config.\n\n## Answer needed\n\nPick a window (A unless told otherwise) and confirm pending tokens are unspendable. A one-line answer closes this.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Decision 1 recorded on this issue: refund/vesting window length chosen (A/B/C/D or an alternative), with the chooser named\n- [ ] Decision 2 recorded on this issue: pending tokens spendable or not, explicitly confirmed\n- [ ] The chosen values are reflected as the config defaults the #2065 build issues read (issue or PR link recorded here), and this issue is closed with the decisions in the closing comment\n",
